<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik Muhammad Jayasi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1477–1542</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Muhammad Jayasi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet from Jais in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> who wrote in the Awadhi language. His epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a>, completed in 1540, tells in allegorical form the story of the Sinhalese princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, queen of Chittor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s desire for her. One of the major works of early Hindi literature, it created the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmini">Padmini</a> that later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> tradition adopted as history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-muhammad-jayasi.jpg" alt="Malik Muhammad Jayasi" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Nagamati_talks_to_her_parrot,_Padmavat,_c1750.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
